ould replace one diagonal with vertical line x=1, which would cover (1,2)-(1,8). But (1,8) is already covered by L2, but vertical line x=1 would also cover (1,7). However, vertical line x=1 also covers (1,1) which is already covered by L1. That's okay. So we could use vertical line x=1 as a non-sunny line. That would cover all points in column 1. Then we could drop diagonal c=3 maybe. Let's try to choose non-sunny lines: vertical x=1, diagonal c=4,5,6,7,9 (that's 6 lines). Let's see if that covers all uncovered points.\n\nVertical x=1 covers (1,b) for b=1..8. So column 1 fully covered.\n\nNow we need to cover other uncovered points:\n\nColumn 2: (2,1) (c=3), (2,3) (c=5), (2,4) (c=6), (2,5) (c=7), (2,7) (c=9). We have diagonals c=5,6,7,9. So (2,1) (c=3) is not covered. Could we cover (2,1) with a horizontal line y=1? That's non-sunny. But we haven't used horizontal lines. We could replace one diagonal with horizontal y=1. But horizontal y=1 would also cover many points: (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1),(6,1),(7,1),(8,1). Some of those are already covered, but that's okay. So we could use horizontal y=1 instead of diagonal c=4 maybe. Let's try to choose non-sunny lines: vertical x=1, horizontal y=1, diagonal c=5,6,7,9. That's 6 lines.\n\nNow check coverage:\n\nVertical x=1 covers column 1.\n\nHorizontal y=1 covers all points with b=1: (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1),(6,1),(7,1),(8,1). So column 2 point (2,1) covered, column 3 point (3,1) covered, column 4 point (4,1) covered, column 5 point (5,1) covered, column 6 point (6,1) covered, column 7 point (7,1) covered, column 8 point (8,1) covered. Good.\n\nNow diagonal c=5 covers (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). (4,1) already covered, but that's fine. So c=5 covers (1,4),(2,3),(3,2).\n\nDiagonal c=6 covers (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). (3,3) covered by L1, (4,2) covered by L2, (5,1) covered by horizontal y=1. So c=6 covers (1,5),(2,4).\n\nDiagonal c=7 covers (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1). (3,4) covered by L2, (6,1) covered by horizontal y=1. So c=7 covers (1,6),(2,5),(4,3),(5,2).\n\nDiagonal c=9 covers (1,8),(2,7),(3,6),(4,5),(5,4),(6,3),(7,2),(8,1). (1,8) covered by L2, (8,1) covered by horizontal y=1. So c=9 covers (2,7),(3,6),(4,5),(5,4),(6,3),(7,2).\n\nNow let's list all points and see if any uncovered remain.\n\nPoints covered by L1: (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(4,4).\n\nPoints covered by L2: (1,8),(2,6),(3,4),(4,2).\n\nVertical x=1: (1,1)-(1,8) (already covered many, but covers all column 1).\n\nHorizontal y=1: (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1),(6,1),(7,1),(8,1).\n\nDiagonal c=5: (1,4),(2,3),(3,2).\n\nDiagonal c=6: (1,5),(2,4).\n\nDiagonal c=7: (1,6),(2,5),(4,3),(5,2).\n\nDiagonal c=9: (2,7),(3,6),(4,5),(5,4),(6,3),(7,2).\n\nNow let's list all points in the triangle (a,b) with a+b ≤9:\n\nWe can systematically list by a:\n\na=1: b=1..8. Covered: b=1 (vertical x=1, horizontal y=1, L1), b=2 (vertical x=1), b=3 (vertical x=1), b=4 (vertical x=1, diagonal c=5), b=5 (vertical x=1, diagonal c=6), b=6 (vertical x=1, diagonal c=7), b=7 (vertical x=1), b=8 (vertical x=1, L2). So all covered.\n\na=2: b=1..7. Covered: b=1 (horizontal y=1), b=2 (L1), b=3 (c=5), b=4 (c=6), b=5 (c=7), b=6 (L2), b=7 (c=9). So all covered.\n\na=3: b=1..6. Covered: b=1 (horizontal y=1), b=2 (c=5), b=3 (L1), b=4 (L2), b=5 (c=9? Actually c=8 would be (3,5) but we didn't have c=8. Wait (3,5) sum=8, diagonal c=8 not used. So (3,5) is not covered yet. Let's check: (3,5) is a point with a=3,b=5, sum=8. Did we cover (3,5) anywhere? Not by L1 (3,3), not by L2 (3,4), not by vertical x=1 (a=1), not by horizontal y=1 (b=1), not by c=5 (points (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)), not by c=6 (points (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1)), not by c=7 (points (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1)), not by c=9 (points (2,7),(3,6),(4,5),(5,4),(6,3),(7,2),(8,1)). So (3,5) is uncovered. Also (3,6) is covered by c=9. So we have uncovered (3,5). Also (3,2) is covered by c=5. So only (3,5) missing.\n\na=4: b=1..5. Covered: b=1 (horizontal y=1), b=2 (L2), b=3 (c=7), b=4 (L1