 Need to cover with 2 sunny lines, impossible.\n\nThus seems k=2 impossible for n=5.\n\nNow n=6: total points = 21. Need 6 lines, exactly 2 sunny, so 4 non-sunny lines. We can try to find a construction similar to n=8 but with fewer non-sunny lines. For n=6, we can try vertical x=1, horizontal y=1, diagonal c=6, diagonal c=7? That's 4 non-sunny lines. Let's see coverage.\n\nPoints for n=6: a+b ≤ 7.\n\nList points:\n\na=1: b=1..6\na=2: b=1..5\na=3: b=1..4\na=4: b=1..3\na=5: b=1..2\na=6: b=1\n\nTotal 21 points.\n\nNon-sunny lines:\n\n- vertical x=1: (1,1)-(1,6)\n- horizontal y=1: (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1),(6,1)\n- diagonal c=6: (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1)\n- diagonal c=7: (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1)\n\nNow coverage:\n\nVertical x=1 covers (1,1)-(1,6).\nHorizontal y=1 covers (1,1)-(6,1).\nDiagonal c=6 covers (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1).\nDiagonal c=7 covers (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1).\n\nNow list all points and see which are uncovered:\n\na=1: b=1-6 all covered by vertical x=1.\n\na=2: b=1 (horizontal), b=2? (2,2) not covered yet. b=3? (2,3) not covered. b=4 (c=6) covered. b=5 (c=7) covered.\n\na=3: b=1 (horizontal), b=2? (3,2) not covered. b=3 (c=6) covered. b=4 (c=7) covered.\n\na=4: b=1 (horizontal), b=2 (c=6) covered, b=3 (c=7) covered.\n\na=5: b=1 (horizontal), b=2 (c=7) covered.\n\na=6: b=1 (horizontal).\n\nThus uncovered points: (2,2),(2,3),(3,2). That's three points. We have two sunny lines left to cover them. As before, any line covering any two of these points is non-sunny: (2,2)-(2,3) horizontal, (2,2)-(3,2) vertical, (2,3)-(3,2) slope = (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not sunny. So each sunny line can cover at most one of these points. So we need at least three sunny lines. So k=2 impossible for n=6 with this selection.\n\nMaybe we can choose different non-sunny lines to reduce uncovered points to at most 2. Let's try different combination: vertical x=1, diagonal c=5, diagonal c=6, diagonal c=7? That's 4 non-sunny lines. Let's see.\n\nDiagonal c=5: (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). Diagonal c=6: (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1). Diagonal c=7: (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1). Vertical x=1: (1,1)-(1,6). Combined coverage:\n\n- vertical x=1: (1,1)-(1,6)\n- diagonal c=5: (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)\n- diagonal c=6: (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1)\n- diagonal c=7: (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1)\n\nNow list uncovered points:\n\na=1: all covered (vertical covers all b=1..6).\n\na=2: b=1? (2,1) not covered (vertical x=2 not used, horizontal not used, diagonal c=5 includes (2,3), c=6 includes (2,4), c=7 includes (2,5). So (2,1) uncovered. b=2? (2,2) not covered. b=3 covered by c=5. b=4 covered by c=6. b=5 covered by c=7.\n\na=3: b=1? (3,1) not covered. b=2 covered by c=5. b=3 covered by c=6. b=4 covered by c=7.\n\na=4: b=1 covered by c=5. b=2 covered by c=6. b=3 covered by c=7.\n\na=5: b=1 covered by c=6. b=2 covered by c=7.\n\na=6: b=1 covered by c=7.\n\nThus uncovered points: (2,1),(2,2),(3,1). That's three points. Again need at least three sunny lines.\n\nTry vertical x=1, horizontal y=1, diagonal c=5, diagonal c=6. That's 4 non-sunny lines. Let's see.\n\nVertical x=1: (1,1)-(1,6)\nHorizontal y=1: (1,1)-(6,1)\nDiagonal c=5: (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)\nDiagonal c=6: (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1)\n\nCoverage:\n\na=1: b=1-6 covered by vertical.\na=2: b=1 (horizontal), b=2? (2,2) not covered. b=3 (c=5). b=4 (c=6). b=5? (2,5) not covered (c=7 not used). So (2,5) uncovered.\na=3: b=1 (horizontal), b=2 (c=5), b=3 (c=6), b=4? (3,4) not covered. So (3,4) uncovered.\na=4: b=1 (c=5), b=2 (c=6), b=3? (4,3) not covered. So (4,3) uncovered.\na=5: b=1 (c=6), b=2? (5,2) not covered. So (5,2) uncovered.\na=6: b=1 (horizontal). So uncovered points: (2,2),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2). That's 5 points. Too many.\n\nThus seems k=2 impossible for n=6.\n\nNow n=7: total points = 28. Need 7 lines, exactly 2 sunny, so 5 non-sunny lines. Let's try to find a construction similar to n=8 but with one fewer non-sunny line. For n=8 we used vertical x=1, horizo